  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe proposed approach leverages convex optimization by constructing a relaxation of the original problem. While the Erd\u0151s problem's bilinear terms make it non-convex, we can approximate it with a linear program by bounding the nonlinear interactions. This approach allows us to use efficient convex solvers and provides bounds on the true optimal value.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nWe introduce auxiliary variables z[i][j] = h[i]*(1 - h[j]) and formulate the problem with linear constraints:\n\nMinimize C5\nSubject to:\n- z[i][j] \u2264 h[i] for all i,j\n- z[i][j] \u2265 0 for all i,j\n- z[i][j] + 1 - h[j] \u2264 1 for all i,j\n- h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2\n\nC5 is the maximum over all shifts k of (2.0 / n_points) * \u2211z[i][i+k], where i+k wraps modulo n_points.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Setup:\n- n_points = 70\n- z = cvx.Variable(n_points, n_points)\n- h = cvx.Variable(n_points)\n\nb. Constraints:\n- z[i][j] >= h[i] + h[j] - 1 (lower bound on product)\n- z[i][j] <= h[i] (upper bound)\n- \u2211h == n_points / 2\n- 0 <= h[i] <=1\n\nc. Objective:\nC5 = (2.0 / n_points) * max over k of \u2211z[i][i+k mod n_points]\n\nd. Solve with CVXOPT\n- Extract optimal h from solution\n- Ensure h \u2208 [0,1], sum(h)=n_points/2\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points =70\n- CVXOPT solver parameters:\n   - max_iter=1000\n   - tolerance=1e-9\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nCVXOPT is efficient for large LPs. Expect to solve within 1100s, with 4900 variables and ~7000 constraints. Feasibility projection ensures valid h.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n- Check h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- Verify sum(h) = n_points/2\n- Evaluate C5 with evaluator\n- Return (h, C5, n_points)",
